  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Ajaykumar vs The Food Inspector on 5 February, 2016",
    "facts": "2.Petitioner is the third accused in the case. He and two others are facing prosecution for offences under Section 16(1)(a)(1) and (ii) r/w 7(1) & 2(19)(a)(b) & (m) of PFA Act, 1954 and Rule 55 of PPA Rules, 1955. 3.The petitioner is a general merchant engaged inter-alia in sale of coriander. It is alleged that samples seized from the petitioner's shop was found adulterated by mixing of sulphur-dioxide with coriander powder.",
    "arguments": "4.Learned counsel for petitioner submits that though the report of the Public Analyst, Chennai, dated 28.10.2005 informed of the sample containing sulphur-dioxide, the complaint had been preferred by the respondent only on 27.06.2006. Notice under Section 13(2) of the Act informing the right of the petitioner to seek analysis of the second sample had been issued only on 28.06.2006. The right of the petitioner/accused to have a second analysis of the sample seized had been rendered nugatory by the fact that the very complaint as also the notice under Section 13(2) of the Act had been issued much after the expiry of the shelf life of the product. In support of such submission, learned counsel relied on the judgment of the Apex Court in Girishbhai Dahyabhai Shah v C.C.Jani and another [JT 2009 (12) SC80]. Paragraph No.8 of such judgment reads as follows: \"8. 5.Learned Government Advocate (Crl.side) submits that after due inspection and seizing of samples in keeping with the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, it had been found that the product sold by the petitioner/accused contained sulphur-dioxide. He would contend that a prima facie case stood made out against the petitioner and he would have to establish his innocence in the course of trial.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "4.Learned counsel for petitioner submits that though the report of the Public Analyst, Chennai, dated 28.10.2005 informed of the sample containing sulphur-dioxide, the complaint had been preferred by the respondent only on 27.06.2006. Notice under Section 13(2) of the Act informing the right of the petitioner to seek analysis of the second sample had been issued only on 28.06.2006. The right of the petitioner/accused to have a second analysis of the sample seized had been rendered nugatory by the fact that the very complaint as also the notice under Section 13(2) of the Act had been issued much after the expiry of the shelf life of the product. In support of such submission, learned counsel relied on the judgment of the Apex Court in Girishbhai Dahyabhai Shah v C.C.Jani and another [JT 2009 (12) SC80]. 5.Learned Government Advocate (Crl.side) submits that after due inspection and seizing of samples in keeping with the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, it had been found that the product sold by the petitioner/accused contained sulphur-dioxide. He would contend that a prima facie case stood made out against the petitioner and he would have to establish his innocence in the course of trial.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "Paragraph No.8 of such judgment reads as follows: \"8."
  },
